TI  - Presumed oxaliplatin-related Guillain-Barre syndrome: A case report and review of 
      chemotherapy-related Guillain-Barre syndrome.
PG  - e51121
LID - 10.1097/MD.0000000000051121 [doi]
AB  - RATIONALE: Oxaliplatin, a platinum-based chemotherapeutic widely used in 
      colorectal, hepatobiliary, and pancreatic malignancies, commonly causes a 
      symmetric, cold-induced sensory neuropathy. Rarely, it has been linked to 
      immune-mediated Guillain-Barre Syndrome (GBS), which can be misattributed to 
      drug-induced sensory neuropathy and delay diagnosis. Distinguishing the two is 
      critical because GBS can progress to respiratory failure and death. PATIENT 
      CONCERNS: A 77-year-old woman with concurrent metastatic cholangiocarcinoma and 
      colorectal carcinoma developed acute, symmetric, ascending motor weakness with 
      clinically preserved sensation approximately 2 weeks after her last cycle of 
      gemcitabine, oxaliplatin, leucovorin, and 5-fluorouracil (GOLF), rapidly 
      progressing to an inability to ambulate. DIAGNOSIS: Neurological examination and 
      cerebrospinal fluid analysis (albuminocytologic dissociation) were consistent 
      with GBS, and nerve conduction studies demonstrated absent motor and sensory 
      responses, indicating a severe axonal variant (AMSAN); sensory involvement was 
      electrodiagnostic rather than clinical. An extensive infectious, autoimmune, and 
      paraneoplastic workup was negative, supporting a diagnosis of presumed 
      oxaliplatin-related GBS; a Naranjo score of 4 indicated a "possible" drug 
      association. INTERVENTIONS: She received 5 sessions of therapeutic plasma 
      exchange followed by 4 sessions of intravenous immunoglobulin. OUTCOMES: No 
      meaningful neurological recovery occurred; she developed progressive respiratory 
      failure requiring mechanical ventilation and died. LESSONS: Clinicians should 
      maintain a high index of suspicion for GBS in patients on platinum-based 
      chemotherapy who develop abrupt, symmetric motor weakness after common causes 
      have been ruled out. Because causality is difficult to establish, the association 
      remains presumed; early recognition, prompt immunotherapy, and pharmacovigilance 
      are essential to reduce mortality.
