TI  - Hepatocellular carcinoma in Turkey: A nationwide health system database analysis 
      of clinical patterns and survival.
PG  - 103255
LID - S1877-7821(26)00271-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.canep.2026.103255 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is a leading cause of cancer-related 
      mortality worldwide. However, comprehensive population-level data describing 
      etiologic patterns, treatment exposure, healthcare utilisation, and survival 
      remain limited in many countries. This study aimed to characterise the national 
      burden, care patterns, and outcomes of HCC in Turkey. METHODS: We performed a 
      nationwide, real-world analysis of adult patients diagnosed with HCC between 2016 
      and 2024 using administrative health data. Cases were identified by an 
      administrative case definition requiring at least three separate records of 
      ICD-10 codes C22, C22.0, or C22.9; at least one C22.0 code was not required, so 
      HCC could not be confirmed at the individual level. Baseline comorbidities were 
      restricted to conditions recorded on or before the date of diagnosis. Demographic 
      characteristics, underlying liver disease, etiologic factors, treatment exposure, 
      and the level and type of diagnosing institution were assessed. Overall survival 
      (OS) was estimated using the Kaplan-Meier method. To reduce immortal-time bias, 
      treatment exposure was analysed in a three-month landmark cohort; because 
      treatment dates were unavailable, this reduces but does not eliminate the bias. 
      Multivariable Cox regression was performed within the same cohort. RESULTS: A 
      total of 13,674 patients (64.6% male; median age 64 years, IQR 55-73) were 
      included, of whom 61.4% had died at the data cutoff; median follow-up was 53.0 
      months. The median OS was 13.2 months (95% CI 12.1-14.5), with 1-, 3-, and 5-year 
      OS of 50.9%, 41.1%, and 36.9%; because patients dying before three qualifying 
      records accumulated could not enter the cohort, these estimates apply to patients 
      meeting the case-identification algorithm and are likely to overstate population 
      survival. OS was markedly longer in women than in men (median 50.9 vs 8.2 
      months), and longer in patients without cirrhosis recorded at diagnosis than in 
      those with cirrhosis (18.5 vs 9.0 months; both P < 0.001). Hepatitis B was the 
      most frequently recorded specific etiology (29.4%); among mutually exclusive 
      categories, NAFLD-associated HCC had the most favourable survival (5-year OS 
      61.0%) and alcohol-related HCC the least (median 8.8 months), while HBV-HDV 
      co-infection was associated with longer survival than HBV mono-infection (58.2 vs 
      12.0 months; P < 0.001), a difference that persisted after adjustment for age, 
      sex, and cirrhosis; no specific etiology could be assigned in 56.1% of patients. 
      Sensitivity analyses restricting etiologic ascertainment to codes recorded within 
      3 months before to 3 months after diagnosis, or incorporating viral serology, 
      attenuated the survival of the specific viral etiologies, indicating 
      survival-dependent ascertainment. In a multivariable Cox model fitted in the 
      three-month landmark cohort (n = 8958), older age, male sex, cirrhosis, and 
      systemic therapy were associated with higher mortality (all P < 0.001), with 
      time-varying associations for age, cirrhosis, and systemic therapy. The 
      association of local therapy with mortality was not constant over follow-up (HR 
      0.78, 95% CI 0.72-0.85 during 0-24 months after the landmark and 1.22, 1.04-1.43 
      thereafter); the overall association was no longer evident after liver 
      transplantation was excluded from the local-therapy category. Care was highly 
      centralised within secondary and tertiary institutions. CONCLUSIONS: In this 
      nationwide analysis, HCC in Turkey was characterised by limited OS and pronounced 
      sex-based disparities, with survival differing by cirrhosis status and recorded 
      etiology; etiologic ascertainment was itself survival-dependent, so these 
      contrasts describe recorded categories. Hepatitis B was the most frequently 
      recorded specific etiology, underscoring the continued importance of HBV 
      prevention, screening, and surveillance, while HCV, HDV, alcohol-related and 
      metabolic liver disease accounted for smaller recorded proportions; temporal 
      trends in etiology were not analysed. Because tumour stage and liver function 
      were unavailable, the treatment-survival associations are descriptive and subject 
      to confounding by indication. Efforts focused on HBV control, earlier detection, 
      risk-based surveillance of patients with cirrhosis, and improved referral 
      pathways may improve population-level outcomes.
