TI  - Associations of food processing and dietary risk factors with colorectal cancer 
      incidence in the NIH-AARP Diet and Health Study: A cohort study.
PG  - e1005251
LID - 10.1371/journal.pmed.1005251 [doi]
LID - e1005251
AB  - BACKGROUND: Ultra-processed foods (UPF) account for >50% of energy intake by US 
      adults. UPF intake can include dietary components linked to lower colorectal 
      cancer (CRC) risk, such as whole grain and dairy products, and others linked to 
      higher CRC risk, such as processed meats, potentially explaining the 
      heterogeneity of evidence for an association between UPF and CRC risk. Our goal 
      was to estimate the associations of UPF and established CRC-related dietary 
      factors, by UPF and non-UPF source, with CRC incidence in a large cohort study. 
      METHODS AND FINDINGS: US adults, aged 50-71, who enrolled in the NIH-AARP Diet 
      and Health Study in 1995-96, self-reported dietary intake using a food frequency 
      questionnaire (FFQ). Median UPF intake was 293 g/1000 kcal/day or 43% of daily 
      energy intake. We assigned disaggregated FFQ items to Nova classification and 
      categorized UPF intake (g/1000 kcal/day) into sex-specific quintiles. 
      Participants who were cancer-free at baseline (N = 461,682) were followed for 
      cancer incidence through 2018, and 10,075 colorectal adenocarcinoma cases were 
      diagnosed. We used multivariable-adjusted Cox proportional hazards regression 
      models, adjusted for demographic, lifestyle, and health-related factors, 
      including age, sex, race/ethnicity, education, physical activity, smoking, and 
      alcohol drinking, to estimate associations for UPF and related dietary components 
      with CRC incidence. UPF intake was not associated with CRC incidence (hazard 
      ratio 5th versus 1st quintile 0.97; 95% confidence interval [0.91-1.03]; 
      ptrend = 0.55). Whole grain, fiber, dairy, and calcium intakes from UPF sources 
      were inversely but meat intake from UPF sources was positively associated with 
      CRC incidence. The main limitations of the study relate to its observational 
      design and include potential residual confounding and exposure misclassification 
      owing to systematic or random error in self-reported dietary data. CONCLUSIONS: 
      Total UPF intake was not associated with incident CRC in this cohort of older, US 
      adults. This may be explained, in part, by opposing associations of some UPF with 
      CRC risk. Our findings support current dietary guidance to consume whole grains, 
      fiber, dairy, and calcium and avoid processed meat for CRC prevention.
