TI  - Maternal deaths on arrival: A 5-year retrospective study in a health district in 
      South Africa.
PG  - e0358988
LID - 10.1371/journal.pone.0358988 [doi]
LID - e0358988
AB  - Maternal deaths on arrival remain one of the blind spots in maternal death 
      surveillance and response programs. There are no policy and guideline addressing 
      maternal deaths on arrival in South Africa. Main objective was to analyse 
      maternal deaths on arrival at the health facilities in a South African district. 
      All maternal deaths on arrival at the health facilities at the Ekurhuleni 
      district over a period of five years and nine months (July 2014 to March 2020), 
      were retrospectively reviewed. Analysis included maternal mortality ratio, 
      demography, gestational age at booking, delivery and deaths, newborn outcome, 
      causes and interval between hospital discharge and presenting as dead. 
      Descriptive statistics (proportion, mean, standard deviation, median and 
      interquartile range) and inferential statistics (Chi-square test, Fishers exact 
      test and Students t test) comparing the women with known and unknown causes, are 
      presented. Twenty-eight (5.72%) women out of the 489 maternal deaths were dead on 
      arrival. The maternal mortality ratio was 7.65 maternal deaths per 100000 live 
      births. Causes of deaths were inconclusive in 17 (60.71%). Among the known 
      causes, the commonest (5, 45.45%) cause was haemorrhage. Median interval between 
      discharge from hospital and presenting as dead was 2.5 days. Information on three 
      delay model were unavailable. Delay two was mentioned for only one year where 
      ambulance service was disrupted due to local protests against municipal services. 
      Comparison between the known and unknown causes showed the regional hospitals had 
      significantly higher numbers of known causes. CONCLUSION: Maternal deaths on 
      arrival was an important contributor to maternal mortality in this study although 
      poorly documented. Majority attended antenatal care and delivered at the 
      hospitals raising a suspicion of early hospital discharge and failure to timely 
      follow-up. A protocol and guideline, with a focus on postmortem examinations 
      should be developed for management of maternal deaths on arrival.
