TI  - Associations Between Maternal Mental Health and Congenital Syphilis Birth 
      Outcomes: Analysis of Georgia Surveillance Data, 2018-2023.
PG  - 807-813
LID - 10.1097/OLQ.0000000000002372 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Congenital syphilis (CS) is a preventable yet persistent public 
      health problem in the United States. Maternal mental health conditions (MMHC) 
      during pregnancy may contribute to missed prevention opportunities, but their 
      association with CS outcomes is unclear. METHODS: Georgia sexually transmitted 
      infections (STI) surveillance data (2018-2023) were analyzed to assess prebirth 
      MMHC mentions among syphilis-affected pregnancies, their association with CS, and 
      variation by maternal household composition as a proxy for immediate social 
      support. MMHC mentions were identified using rule-based analysis of free-text 
      case narratives from the year before delivery. Modified Poisson regression was 
      used to estimate adjusted prevalence ratios (aPR), and an internal validation 
      substudy informed probabilistic bias adjustment for MMHC misclassification. 
      RESULTS: Among 1684 syphilis-affected pregnancies, 416 (25%) resulted in CS. 
      Prebirth MMHC were documented in 4% and were associated with higher CS prevalence 
      (aPR, 1.5; 95% confidence interval [CI], 1.1-2.1). Although the association did 
      not differ significantly by maternal household composition, it was attenuated 
      among women living with family or partners (aPR, 1.18; 95% CI, 0.57-2.47) 
      compared with those living alone or with nonfamily members (aPR, 1.42; 95% CI, 
      0.70-2.90). Internal validation indicated MMHC under-ascertainment in 
      surveillance data; bias-adjusted analyses suggested a stronger association with 
      CS, albeit with considerable uncertainty (bias-adjusted PR, 2.1; 95% simulation 
      interval, 0.8-5.0). CONCLUSIONS: Prebirth MMHC mentions captured during STI case 
      investigations were associated with increased CS prevalence and may serve as an 
      early indicator of vulnerability. Incorporating mental health-informed follow-up 
      into STI surveillance workflows could enhance care coordination and strengthen CS 
      prevention.
