TI  - Neighborhood socioeconomic status in relation to mortality rates of 
      gastrointestinal cancers among Asian American, Native Hawaiian, and Pacific 
      Islander populations in California.
LID - 10.1007/s10552-026-02261-x [doi]
LID - 176
AB  - OBJECTIVE: Gastrointestinal (GI) cancers account for approximately 24% of all 
      cancer deaths in the United States (US). Demographic and socioeconomic status 
      (SES) factors have been associated with mortality in GI cancers. However, the 
      associations between GI cancer mortality and neighborhood SES (nSES) among 
      non-Hispanic Asian American, Native Hawaiian, and Pacific Islander (AANHPI) 
      populations in California have not been investigated. This study examined GI 
      cancer mortality rates by nSES among AANHPI adults in California. METHODS: The 
      California Cancer Registry, part of the population-based Surveillance, 
      Epidemiology and End Results (SEER) program of the National Cancer Institute, was 
      used to identify AANHPI males and females who died of GI cancers from 2008 
      through 2012. GI cancer deaths were defined by the following sites: colorectum, 
      stomach, esophagus, small intestine, and anus or anal canal. Each death was 
      assigned a nSES index using the 2010 US Census data. Age-adjusted mortality rates 
      of GI cancer and mortality rate ratios (MRR) as well as 95% confidence intervals 
      by nSES quintiles were calculated for a 5-year period from 2008 through 2012. 
      Subgroup analyses were conducted by sex and cancer site. RESULTS: A total of 
      5,153 cases were included in the final study population for analysis. Among all 
      AANHPI males and females, those residing in the lowest nSES quintile had a 
      1.31-fold higher MRR of GI cancers compared to those in the highest nSES quintile 
      (Q1 vs. Q5; 95% confidence interval (CI): 1.19-1.45). This positive association 
      was observed in both males and females, although not statistically significant in 
      females (males: nSES Q1 vs. Q5 MRR: 1.50; 95% CI: 1.31-1.71; females: nSES Q1 vs. 
      Q5 MRR: 1.11; 95% CI: 0.96-1.29). Similar patterns of associations were seen 
      across specific GI cancer sites. CONCLUSION: AANHPI adults in California residing 
      in lower nSES neighborhoods experienced higher GI cancer mortality compared with 
      those in higher nSES areas. Future studies are needed to clarify the underlying 
      mechanisms and identify actionable strategies to mitigate nSES-related inequities 
      in GI cancer outcomes in the AANHPI population.
