OAB - We used data from a group of people in Canada between 45 and 85 years old who are 
      followed every 3 years or until they die to understand how changes in alcohol use 
      affect the risk of death in people with and without fatty liver disease. We found 
      that alcohol use, liver health and overall lifestyle factors can change over 
      time. Also, people with fatty liver disease who drank the most alcohol had the 
      greatest risk of death. Our findings suggest that accounting for changes in 
      alcohol use, liver fat and other indicators of health over time provides a more 
      accurate understanding of long-term health risks than relying on a single 
      measurement.
