TI  - Implementing a Risk-Stratified National Breast Screening Programme in the United 
      Kingdom: A Model-Based Cost-effectiveness Analysis.
PG  - 1752-1762
LID - 10.1158/1055-9965.EPI-26-0103 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Risk-stratified breast screening, in which women are offered 
      different screening intervals based on their breast cancer risk, has been 
      proposed as a way to improve the benefits of screening in the United Kingdom 
      National Breast Screening Programme (UK-NBSP). The aim of this study was to 
      explore the cost-effectiveness of potential risk-stratified breast screening 
      strategies in the UK-NBSP. METHODS: An existing validated discrete event 
      simulation model (MANC-RISK-SCREEN) was used to evaluate the cost-effectiveness 
      of 3 risk-stratified NBSPs (risk 1, risk 2, and risk 3) compared with the current 
      UK-NBSP, 2-yearly screening, and no screening [lifetime horizon; health service 
      perspective; costs ( pound; at 2024 prices)]. MANC-RISK-SCREEN was modified to account 
      for bias in risk prediction and allocation to risk categories (risk 
      misclassification). Uncertainty in the results due to model inputs was quantified 
      using probabilistic sensitivity analysis and one-way sensitivity analyses. 
      RESULTS: At a cost-effectiveness threshold of  pound20,000 per quality-adjusted life 
      year, 2-yearly screening was most likely to be cost-effective, followed by a 
      risk-stratified approach whereby women at high risk (>8% 10-year risk) were 
      screened annually and women at moderate risk (5%-8% 10-year risk) biannually. 
      When risk misclassification was accounted for, the ranking of risk-stratified 
      screening strategies improved relative to universal strategies. CONCLUSIONS: If 
      2-yearly screening is infeasible due to resource constraints, implementing a 
      risk-stratified approach in the existing NBSP in the United Kingdom would be a 
      cost-effective use of healthcare resources. IMPACT: Implementation of 
      risk-stratified breast screening in the UK national screening program has the 
      potential to improve cancer detection at a reasonable additional resource use and 
      cost.
