TI  - Second Primary Malignancy Risk in Patients with Multiple Myeloma Receiving CAR 
      T-cell Therapy or Other Systemic Anticancer Treatments: A Real-World Comparative 
      Study.
PG  - 4178-4187
LID - 10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-25-4068 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE: This study aims to compare the risk of second primary malignancy (SPM) 
      between patients with multiple myeloma who received chimeric antigen receptor 
      T-cell (CAR T) therapy versus other systemic anticancer therapies (SACT). 
      EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN: Adult patients with multiple myeloma who initiated CAR T 
      therapy or other SACT were identified from Komodo Health claims data and weighted 
      to balance baseline characteristics. Cumulative incidence of SPM was estimated 
      over 24 months, and P values were calculated for the difference between 0 and 24 
      months. RESULTS: The study included 435 patients who received CAR T therapy and 
      12,268 patients who received other SACT (median follow-up, 11.8 months). Compared 
      with other SACT, CAR T therapy was associated with similar risks of any SPM (at 
      24 months, 24.1% vs. 22.3%; P0-24 months = 0.31) and solid SPM (9.1% vs. 11.5%, 
      P0-24 months = 0.32) but significantly higher risk of hematologic SPM (17.9% vs. 
      13.1%, P0-24 months = 0.04). In a sensitivity analysis requiring >/= 2 claims to 
      identify an SPM, difference in hematologic SPM risk was attenuated (5.5% vs. 
      4.9%, P0-24 months = 0.08). Notably, bone marrow examinations were more common 
      after CAR T therapy (e.g., 47% vs. 13% at 0-3 months). CONCLUSIONS: In this 
      real-world dataset with relatively short follow-up, patients with multiple 
      myeloma seemed to have a higher risk of hematologic SPM after CAR T therapy 
      compared with other SACT. However, misclassification and detection bias cannot be 
      ruled out. The association warrants further evaluation. Physicians should be 
      vigilant for myeloid malignancies after CAR T therapy.
