TI  - Assessment of mpox severity and associated factors in Uganda, November 
      2024-February 2025.
PG  - 108832
LID - S1201-9712(26)00467-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ijid.2026.108832 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: In this study, we characterized the clinical severity of 
      laboratory-confirmed mpox case-patients admitted in Mpox isolation and treatment 
      units (MTUs) and identified factors associated with severity in Uganda from 
      November 2024 to February 2025. METHODS: We used the mpox severity scoring system 
      (MPSSS) tool to assess severity, a 7-parameter tool with each question assigned 
      scores of 0-4 and summed up with totals ranging from 1 to 23. Total scores >/=10 
      indicated "severe disease." We fitted modified Poisson regression to identify 
      factors associated with severe disease. RESULTS: Among the 244 case-patients, 128 
      (52%) were male, and the median age was 29 years. In total, 148 (61%) presented 
      with severe disease, and 74 (30%) had underlying conditions; 65 (88%) had HIV. 
      Among those with HIV, 58 (89%) had severe disease compared to 73 (47%) without 
      HIV (adjusted prevalence ratio = 2.3, 95% CI = 1.6-3.3) after adjusting for age 
      and pregnancy. CONCLUSION: The majority of mpox case-patients and nearly all with 
      HIV admitted to MTUs had severe mpox disease. Case-patients with underlying 
      conditions, especially, HIV, could be considered for inpatient care and mpox 
      vaccination to optimize their care and protect them from severe disease.
