TI  - SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated tumor in the oral cavity: a rare case report 
      and review of the literature.
PG  - e41-e46
LID - S2212-4403(26)00155-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.oooo.2026.04.019 [doi]
AB  - SMARCA4-deficient undifferentiated tumor (SMARCA4-UT) is a rare and aggressive 
      neoplasm in the oral and maxillofacial region. To our knowledge, only a single 
      case of SMARCA4-UT involving the oral cavity has been reported, representing 
      metastatic disease from a pulmonary primary. We report a case of a 66-year-old 
      man who presented with a rapidly enlarging mass involving the left posterior 
      maxillary mucosa with underlying bone destruction. Histopathologic examination 
      revealed diffuse sheets of undifferentiated tumor cells with ovoid to epithelioid 
      morphology. Immunohistochemistry demonstrated complete loss of nuclear SMARCA4 
      (BRG1) expression, supporting the diagnosis of SMARCA4-UT. The patient died 
      approximately 10 days after biopsy, precluding further evaluation to definitively 
      exclude metastatic disease. This case highlights the importance of recognizing 
      SMARCA4-UT in the oral cavity to avoid misclassification and initiate prompt and 
      appropriate clinical management, including assessment for metastatic disease.
