TI  - Pain and Kidney Disease Risk in Older Adults: CHARLS Longitudinal Study.
PG  - e1010-e1016
LID - S1524-9042(26)00144-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.pmn.2026.04.008 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE: Kidney disease is a global public health concern, with prevalence 
      increasing among older adults. Early identification of high-risk older adult 
      populations is crucial for timely intervention and treatment. This study utilized 
      data from the China Health and Retirement Longitudinal Study to systematically 
      assess the association between multi-site pain and kidney disease in older 
      adults. DESIGN: A national prospective longitudinal cohort study designed to 
      investigate the association between baseline pain exposure and subsequent kidney 
      disease outcomes. METHODS: A total of 13,062 participants from the 2011 baseline 
      were followed across five waves until 2020. Baseline pain symptoms from Wave 1 
      were analyzed for their association with kidney disease occurrence, defined as 
      diagnosis either at baseline or during follow-up. Pain symptoms included 
      headache, back pain, chest pain, and abdominal pain. Based on multivariable 
      regression analyses, a pain diagnostic score was constructed by assigning weights 
      to different pain sites. RESULTS: Results showed that headache, back pain, chest 
      pain, and abdominal pain were consistently associated with kidney disease across 
      multiple waves. The pain score was positively correlated with kidney disease 
      prevalence (area under the curve = 0.637), and higher scores corresponded to 
      progressively increased risk, up to 3.7 times the overall prevalence, indicating 
      its potential diagnostic value in early screening. CONCLUSION: This study 
      proposes a simple, pain-based score as a potential risk-alert tool for kidney 
      disease in older adults, suggesting its utility in prompting further clinical 
      evaluation. The pain diagnostic score provides a potentially simple and effective 
      tool for early screening of high-risk individuals, supporting timely intervention 
      and possibly slowing disease progression. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: This pain-based 
      risk score provides a low-cost, easily implementable screening tool for kidney 
      disease in older adults, particularly suitable for primary care and 
      resource-limited settings. It enables rapid risk stratification to prioritize 
      targeted renal function testing, and also reminds clinicians to screen for 
      underlying renal impairment before prescribing nephrotoxic analgesics such as 
      NSAIDs to elderly patients with chronic pain.
