TI  - Limits of Correctional Health Reporting: Findings From a Nationwide Jail 
      Mortality Survey (2019-2024).
PG  - 240-246
LID - 10.1177/10783458261425158 [doi]
AB  - This article presents findings from a national survey of 287 counties across 43 
      states assessing jail mortality between 2019 and 2024 in an attempt to compare 
      private health care provider outcomes with county providers. The findings 
      demonstrated that currently available information cannot serve as a reliable 
      measure of health care quality in correctional settings due to sociocultural 
      reporting constraints. Institutional and cultural deterrents to objective 
      reporting have led to incomplete records, misclassified causes of death, and 
      failures to maintain legally mandated death reports. There is a clear need for 
      health benchmarking and clear definitions for reporting in correctional health 
      care to enable valid comparisons between health care provider models. The paper 
      draws attention to a recently established correctional health care-specific 
      patient safety organization as a potential solution to enable correctional health 
      care providers to better understand the drivers of health care outcomes in 
      correctional facilities.
