TI  - Potential Misclassification of Adults Who Vape Cannabis as Adults Who Vape 
      Nicotine and their Associated Sociodemographic and Smoking Characteristics: 
      Results from Wave 5 of the Population Assessment of Tobacco and Health (PATH) 
      Study.
PG  - 817-823
LID - 10.1093/ntr/ntaf233 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE: People use similar devices to vape nicotine and cannabis, potentially 
      causing misclassification error in surveys. This study aimed to (1) describe the 
      degree of misclassification of people who vape cannabis as people who used 
      electronic nicotine products (ENPs) in the past 30-days (P30D); and, (2) describe 
      sociodemographic and tobacco use characteristics among people who vaped cannabis. 
      METHODS: Data were drawn from the Population Assessment of Tobacco and Health 
      Study Wave 5 (2018-19). People who used ENPs in the P30D and stated that they 
      used cannabis with an ENP (n = 5723) were asked how often this occurred ("every 
      time", "most of the time", "sometimes", "rarely", "never"). We report weighted 
      prevalence estimates of cannabis use frequency by sociodemographic 
      characteristics and cigarette smoking status. RESULTS: Among adults, 8.9% [95% 
      confidence interval (CI): 8.62-9.24] used ENPs in the P30D; among ENP consumers, 
      9.1% (95% CI: 8.23-10.05) used cannabis "every time." Excluding "every time" 
      cannabis use, P30D ENP use was 7.7% (95% CI: 7.44-8.02). People who did not smoke 
      tobacco cigarettes were more likely to vape cannabis "every time" (11.4%; 95% CI: 
      9.82-13.22) compared to people who previously (7.0%; 95% CI: 5.29-9.15) or 
      currently (8.3%; 95% CI: 7.02-9.71) smoked cigarettes. Among people who used ENPs 
      in the P30D, those who used cannabis "every time" had greater odds of being 
      younger and identifying as lesbian, gay, bisexual, or something else, Black, or 
      Hispanic. CONCLUSION: Improved methods are needed to differentiate between 
      nicotine vaping and other types of vaping. Additionally, there is the potential 
      for a greater degree of misclassification by individual characteristics. 
      IMPLICATIONS: People who vape nicotine may also vape cannabis, but there has been 
      little work to-date to understand how accurately surveys differentiate nicotine 
      from cannabis vaping. Though the Population Assessment of Tobacco and Health 
      Study survey clearly asks about nicotine use, 9.1% of people classified as using 
      ENPs in the past 30-day ENP indicated that they were vaping cannabis "every time" 
      with an ENP. The degree of misclassification of ENP use varied by 
      sociodemographic and tobacco use characteristics. To fully understand ENP use, it 
      is necessary to assess cannabis vaping. Failure to disentangle vaped nicotine 
      from cannabis can over- or under-estimate these respective behaviors.
