TI  - Causes of intrauterine fetal death (IUFD) in term singleton pregnancies 
      (2014-2023): a retrospective study.
PG  - 2564285
LID - 10.1080/07853890.2025.2564285 [doi]
LID - 2564285
AB  - BACKGROUND: The risk factors of intrauterine fetal death (IUFD) at term remain 
      uncertain. The cause of death is often difficult to accurately define. This study 
      analyzed the clinical characteristics and the causes of stillbirth at a tertiary 
      maternity center over a 10-year period. METHODS: A retrospective study included 
      70 women with unexpected intrauterine fetal death (IUFD) in singleton term 
      pregnancies (2014 - 2023) at Women's Hospital, Zhejiang University School of 
      Medicine. Cases with fetal anomalies or intrapartum death were excluded. Fetal 
      and maternal variables were analyzed to assess the possible causes of death. 
      RESULTS: The majority of mothers were aged 21-34 and of normal weight. Most 
      deliveries occurred before or at 38 weeks . Umbilical cord abnormalities were 
      present in 72.9% of cases. Among the causes of IUFD, unknown causes predominated 
      (61.4%, n = 43), followed by umbilical cord abnormalities (27.1%, n = 19), with 
      hypercoiling being the most frequent specific finding (28.6%, n = 20). Placental 
      abruption accounted for 7.1%. Reduced fetal movement was reported in 61.4% of 
      cases. The high rate of unknown causes must be interpreted in the context of 
      limited placental pathology evaluation (available in only 47.1% of cases). 
      CONCLUSION: The high proportion of unexplained term stillbirths (61.4%) primarily 
      reflects limited diagnostic investigation, particularly the lack of comprehensive 
      placental pathology. Umbilical cord abnormalities, notably hypercoiling (28.6%), 
      were the most common identifiable cause. Implementing standardized placental 
      examination protocols is critical to elucidate true causes. Monitoring fetal 
      movement remains vital, and enhanced detection of cord abnormalities may aid 
      intervention.
