TI  - Prevalence of pressure injuries and bruising in patients with acute ischemic 
      stroke: A prospective observational study.
PG  - 111465
LID - S0967-5868(25)00438-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jocn.2025.111465 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Skin lesions are associated with higher mortality rates, more 
      extended hospital stays, and poor quality of life. Patients with stroke are at 
      increased risk of developing skin lesions. While significant research exists on 
      skin lesions in critically ill patients, nursing home populations, and the 
      general population, limited studies focus on the prevalence of pressure injury 
      and bruises in patients with stroke. AIM: To evaluate the prevalence and risk 
      factors for pressure injuries, and bruises in ischemic stroke patients. PATIENTS 
      AND METHODS: This prospective study adopted a comprehensive approach. All 
      patients with ischemic stroke admitted to a stroke unit were invited to 
      participate. A nurse and study researcher assessed the dermatological conditions 
      of 503 patients admitted between Jan 1, 2023, and Jun 30, 2024, from admission 
      until discharge. We recorded the time of skin lesion onset pre- 
      (community-acquired) and post-admission (hospital onset) and categorized bruises 
      with unknown causes as unexplained bruises. RESULTS: Skin lesions were identified 
      in 56.7 % (285/503) of patients with ischemic stroke. Factors associated with an 
      increased risk of skin lesions included older age [72.2 +/- 10.9 vs. 67.0 +/- 10.9 
      (p < 0.01)], hypertension [75.7 % vs. 61.9 % (p < 0.01)], cardiovascular disease 
      [20 % vs. 12.8 % (p = 0.04)], atrial fibrillation [12.6 % vs. 6.4 % (p = 0.02)], 
      and stroke severity (p < 0.01). Skin lesions were also linked to more extended 
      hospital stays [12.9 +/- 9.3 vs. 8.0 +/- 7.1 (p < 0.01)] and poorer functional 
      outcomes at discharge (p < 0.01). Pressure injuries (PIs) were found in 25 % 
      (126/503) of patients. Bruises were present in 25.8 % (130/503), with 55.4 % 
      (72/130) community-acquired and 44.6 % (58/130) hospital onset. In 17.7 % 
      (23/130) of cases, the causes of bruises were unknown and defined as unexplained 
      bruises. Binary logistic regression analysis indicated that stroke severity 
      increases the risk of bruises. CONCLUSION: Pressure injuries and bruises are 
      common in ischemic stroke patients. Preadmission PIs are common, and bruises may 
      develop both before and during hospitalization. Pressure injuries can be 
      prevented by implementing prevention strategies. Further research is urgently 
      needed to develop strategies to prevent bruising in stroke patients.
