PMC - PMC12125752
OAB - While some countries have seen a drop in stillbirth and neonatal deaths due to 
      better medical care, Jordan still struggles with high rates. The lack of detailed 
      information about why these deaths happen has slowed progress there. The study 
      aimed to figure out how often these deaths occur, why they happen, and what 
      factors increase the risk in Jordan. To do this, we looked at data from five big 
      hospitals in Jordan between May 2019 and December 2020. We found that out of 
      31,106 babies born to 29,592 women, there were 10.5 stillbirths per 1000 births 
      and 14.2 neonatal deaths per 1000 births. Most neonatal deaths occurred in the 
      first week, mainly due to issues like breathing and heart problems, low birth 
      weight, and birth defects. About a third of stillbirths didn't have a specific 
      cause listed. The study showed that while the rates of these deaths have stayed 
      about the same in Jordan, some reasons behind them can be prevented. We suggest 
      focusing on providing specialized care for high-risk pregnant women and babies 
      born with low birth weight or breathing issues to help lower these numbers.
