TI  - Do cutaneous human papillomavirus genotypes affect head and neck cancer? Evidence 
      and bias-correction from a case-control study.
PG  - 102205
LID - S1877-7821(22)00110-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.canep.2022.102205 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Three genera of human papillomavirus (HPV) infect the oral cavity and 
      oropharynx- alpha (alpha), beta (beta) and gamma (gamma). While alpha-HPV infection is an 
      established risk factor for head and neck cancers (HNC), the role of other genera 
      remains unclear. We aimed to estimate the effect of alpha-, beta-, gamma-HPV on HNC using a 
      hospital-based case-control study. METHODS: We recruited incident HNC cases (396) 
      and controls (439), frequency-matched by age and sex from four main referral 
      hospitals in Montreal, Canada. We collected information on sociodemographic and 
      behavior characteristics using in-person interviews, and tested rinse, brush and 
      tumor specimens for HPV genotypes. We estimated adjusted odds ratios (aOR) and 
      95% confidence intervals (CI) for the effect of HPV on HNC using logistic 
      regression, adjusting for confounding. We conducted probabilistic bias analysis 
      to account for potential exposure misclassification, selection bias, and residual 
      confounding. RESULTS: alpha-HPV genus had a strong effect on HNC, particularly HPV16 
      (aOR=22.6; 95% CI: 10.8, 47.2). beta-HPV was more common among controls (aOR=0.80; 
      95% 0.57, 1.11). After adjustment for HPV16, we found weaker evidence for gamma-HPV 
      (aOR= 1.29; 95% CI: 0.80, 2.08). Combined bias analyses for HPV16 increased the 
      strength of the point estimate, but added imprecision (aOR=54.2, 95% CI: 10.7, 
      385.9). CONCLUSIONS: alpha-HPV, especially HPV16, appears to increase the risk for 
      HNC, while there is little evidence for an effect of beta- or gamma-HPV. beta-HPV may have 
      a preventive effect, while gamma-HPV may increase the risk of HNC, although to a 
      lesser extent than that of alpha-HPV. Results for cutaneous HPV were imprecise and 
      less conclusive. Due to possible epidemiologic biases, the true relation between 
      HPV and HNC could be underestimated in the literature. Further improvement in 
      current methods and more studies of the biologic mechanisms of the three genera 
      in HNC development are warranted.
