DP  - 2022 Jun
TI  - Maternal major depression disorder misclassification errors: Remedies for valid 
      individual- and population-level inference.
PG  - e2614
LID - 10.1002/brb3.2614 [doi]
LID - e2614
AB  - Individual and population level inference about risk and burden of MDD, 
      particularly maternal MDD, is often made using case-finding tools that are 
      imperfect and prone to misclassification error (i.e. false positives and 
      negatives). These errors or biases are rarely accounted for and lead to 
      inappropriate clinical decisions, inefficient allocation of scarce resources, and 
      poor planning of maternal MDD prevention and treatment interventions. The 
      argument that the use of existing maternal MDD case-finding instruments results 
      in misclassification errors is not new; in fact, it has been argued for decades, 
      but by and large its implications and particularly how to correct for these 
      errors for valid inference is unexplored. Correction of the estimates of maternal 
      MDD prevalence, case-finding tool sensitivity and specificity is possible and 
      should be done to inform valid individual and population-level inferences.
