TI  - In Their Own Words: How Trans Women Acquired HIV Infection.
PG  - 2091-2098
LID - 10.1007/s10461-021-03555-8 [doi]
AB  - Despite high HIV prevalence, the reasons trans women acquire HIV are not well 
      understood. Trans women are often mis-classified or aggregated with men who have 
      sex with men (MSM) in epidemiologic studies and HIV surveillance data. Trans 
      women enrolled in the 2019/2020 National HIV Behavioral Surveillance Study in San 
      Francisco were asked an open-ended question about how they were infected with 
      HIV. The most common responses were "Sex with a straight cisgender man partner 
      when the respondent identified as a trans woman" (43.0%); "Sexual assault" 
      (13.9%); "Injection drug use (IDU)" (10.1%); "IDU or sexual contact" (7.6%) and 
      "Sex with a partner who injected drugs" (7.6%). Sex with a cisgender man partner 
      prior to identifying as a trans women (MSM contact) was not mentioned by any 
      respondent. HIV prevention strategies targeting MSM will fail to reach trans 
      women and many of their cisgender men partners.
