      mortality decreased more sharply in women (-2.2%; 95% CI: -2.5; -1.9) than in men 
      (-1.7%; 95% CI: - 1.9; -1.4) and more in the capital cities (-3.8%; 95% CI: - 
      4.3; -3.3) than in other municipalities (-1.5%; 95% CI: - 1.8; -1.3). Regional 
      inequalities were observed, with an increase for men living in other 
      municipalities of the North (3.3; 95% CI: 1.3; 5.4) and Northeast (1.3%; 95% CI: 
      1.0; 1.6). Statistical significance level was set at 5%. Mortality rates after 
      corrections showed a significant difference in relation to the estimates without 
      corrections, mainly due to the redistribution of garbage codes. CONCLUSIONS: 
      Although AMI-related mortality has decreased in Brazil in recent years, this 
      trend is uneven by region and gender. Correcting the numbers of deaths is 
      essential to obtaining more reliable estimates.
