TI  - Assessment of IgE-mediated food allergies in children with atopic dermatitis.
PG  - 77-81
LID - S0301-0546(16)30106-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.aller.2016.06.006 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Atopic dermatitis (AD) is an inflammatory disease of the skin, which 
      is characterised by a chronic relapsing course. AIM: The aim of the study was to 
      assign the prevalence of clinically active food allergies among a group of 
      children between 3 months and 7 years of age, with AD. METHODS: Eighty-eight 
      children with AD were screened for specific IgE antibodies to food proteins. All 
      patients with AD and specific IgE antibodies to food proteins were subjected to 
      Oral Food Challenges (OFCs) with the relevant foods. RESULTS: Food-sensitised 
      patients with moderate levels of sIgE had clinically active food allergy to milk 
      (39.28%) and egg (42.34%) on the basis of positive OFCs. High IgE and 
      eosinophilia had a prevalence of almost 80% and 25%, regardless of concomitant 
      food sensitisation and disease severity. CONCLUSIONS: In this study, clinically 
      active food allergies were recognised in 26.13% of children with AD. 
      Nevertheless, no association was confirmed between food sensitisation and AD 
      severity. High IgE and peripheral eosinophilia have not been found more prevalent 
      among children with severe AD nor among children with food sensitisation. Infants 
      and younger children with AD should be screened for an underlying food allergy, 
      regardless of disease severity.
