TI  - Cutaneous and systemic mastocytosis in children: a risk factor for anaphylaxis?
PG  - 22
LID - 10.1007/s11882-015-0525-1 [doi]
AB  - Childhood mastocytosis is usually a clonal mast cell disease related to 
      activating mutations in KIT. The symptoms in childhood mastocytosis are typically 
      cutaneous in nature although systemic symptoms including anaphylaxis due to the 
      release of mast cells (MC) mediators can also manifest. The prevalence of 
      anaphylaxis reported in childhood mastocytosis is higher than the rates reported 
      in the pediatric general population, but lower than the prevalence of anaphylaxis 
      described in adult mastocytosis. An extensive cutaneous involvement was reported 
      as a risk factor for anaphylaxis, and patients with diffuse cutaneous 
      mastocytosis have been documented to have more severe anaphylaxis symptoms. 
      Anaphylaxis due to unknown causes or idiopathic anaphylaxis was the primary 
      etiology in pediatric mastocytosis, followed by foods as the most relevant 
      identified trigger; however, hymenoptera stings are not a frequent trigger of 
      anaphylaxis in children with mastocytosis in contrast to the adult patients.
