TI  - Challenging identification of a novel PiISF and the rare PiMmaltonZ 
      alpha1-antitrypsin deficiency variants in two patients.
PG  - 742-6
LID - 10.1309/AJCPR7EIQS8PIMLV [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVES: alpha1-Antitrypsin (AAT) deficiency is associated with an increased risk 
      for lung and liver disease. Identification of AAT deficiency as the underlying 
      cause of these diseases is important in correct patient management. METHODS: AAT 
      deficiency is commonly diagnosed by demonstrating low concentrations of AAT 
      followed by genotype and/or phenotype testing. However, this algorithm may miss 
      novel AAT phenotypes. RESULTS: We report two cases of AAT deficiency in two 
      patients: a case of the novel phenotype PiISF, misclassified as PiII by 
      phenotyping, and a case of the rare phenotype PiMmaltonZ misclassified as PiM2Z. 
      CONCLUSIONS: These cases highlight the importance of understanding the 
      limitations of a commonly used diagnostic algorithm, use of further gene 
      sequencing in applicable cases, and the potential for underdiagnosis of AAT 
      deficiency in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.
