VI  - 32
IP  - 4
DP  - 2014 May
TI  - Does increasing life expectancy affect competing mortality after radical 
      prostatectomy?
PG  - 413-8
LID - S1078-1439(13)00446-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.urolonc.2013.10.006 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVES: In Germany, between 1991 to 1993 and 2004 to 2006, the life 
      expectancy in men increased by 4.2 years with greater gains in the eastern part 
      of the country. In this study, we investigated to which degree this 
      life-expectancy increase was translated into lower competing mortality rates 
      after radical prostatectomy. METHODS AND MATERIALS: The study sample comprised 
      6,831 consecutive patients who underwent radical prostatectomy at the Department 
      of Urology of the Dresden University of Technology and the Department of Urology 
      of the University of Hamburg/Martini Clinic in the years 1992 to 2005. The median 
      age was 63.0 years and the median follow-up was 8.6 years. Three time periods 
      (1992-1995, 1996-2001, and 2002-2005) were compared. Competing mortality was the 
      study end point. Rates after 8 years were used for comparison. Comparisons of 
      mortality rates were made with the 2-sided Wald test. RESULTS: The 8-year 
      competing mortality rates decreased from 7.6% (1992-1995) via 5.6% (1996-2001) to 
      4.7% (2002-2005 vs. 1992-1995; P = 0.0127). When deaths due to unknown causes (92 
      of 969 deaths) were considered as non-prostate cancer competing deaths, the 
      corresponding figures were 9.0 % (1992-1995), 6.5% (1996-2001), and 5.6% 
      (2002-2005) vs. 1992 to 1995; P = 0.0107. The effect was greater in men who are 
      65 years of age or older and those of East German origin. CONCLUSIONS: Parallel 
      to the increasing life expectancy in Germany, the competing mortality after 
      radical prostatectomy decreased in both centers mainly in men who are 65 years of 
      age or older. This information may be important for elderly men considering 
      alternatives to immediate curative treatment for early prostate cancer.
