DP  - 2013 May
TI  - Discrepancies in death certificates, public health registries, and judicial 
      determinations in Italy.
PG  - 705-10
LID - 10.1111/1556-4029.12114 [doi]
AB  - The death certificate is mandated by civil law and serves as a medical-scientific 
      document useful for biostatistics and epidemiological research. For a variety of 
      reasons, death certificates can be misclassified. We reviewed data from 
      self-inflicted deaths collected over an 8-year period by the Forensic Institute 
      of the University Sacro Cuore of Rome (Italy). Four hundred and thirty-five of 
      2904 were classified as self-inflicted deaths (15%). The comparison with death 
      certificates processed by the local public health authority (ASL) and by the 
      Italian National Census Bureau (Istat) and with the judicial investigation 
      results available in the Italian Penal Court archive shows some discrepancies. 
      One-hundred and twenty-four of 435 deaths were not considered to be 
      self-inflicted but due to a crime (29% overrecording suicide) with a higher 
      reduction for women, suggesting that it is easier to confuse a murder for suicide 
      in female cases. Any discrepancies between the mortality and crime data are 
      discussed in details.
