DP  - 2013 Feb
TI  - KSHV/HHV8-negative effusion-based lymphoma, a distinct entity associated with 
      fluid overload states.
PG  - 241-9
LID - 10.1097/PAS.0b013e318267fabc [doi]
AB  - Human herpesvirus-8 (HHV8)-positive effusion-based lymphomas have been termed 
      primary effusion lymphoma (PEL) in the WHO Classification of Tumours of 
      Haematopoietic and Lymphoid Tissues. Kaposi sarcoma herpesvirus 
      (KSHV)/HHV8-negative effusion-based lymphomas (KSHV/HHV8-negative EBLs) 
      resembling PELs have been reported in the literature and in many cases have been 
      (mis)classified as PEL-like lymphomas. Herein, we present a series of cases and a 
      review of KSHV/HHV8-negative EBLs. This lymphoma, although cytomorphologically 
      resembling PEL, is a distinct entity with characteristic clinical and pathologic 
      features. Patients are older, generally human immunodeficiency virus negative and 
      not immunosuppressed, frequently hepatitis C positive compared with the 
      population baseline, and often have an underlying medical condition leading to 
      fluid overload. The lymphoma cells express pan-B-cell antigens in 86.7%, and CD20 
      is expressed in 71.1% of the cases. The lymphoma is often of germinal center B or 
      mixed germinal center B/activated B-cell signature with the Hans classifier, and 
      Epstein-Barr virus is positive in nearly 30% of cases. Rare T-cell lymphomas were 
      also reported. Clinical outcomes and response to therapy, including isolated 
      aspiration, are relatively favorable compared with cases of PEL. We suggest that 
      HHV8-negative effusion-based lymphoma is a distinct entity associated with fluid 
      overload states.
