TI  - Abnormal tau deposition in neurons, but not in glial cells in the cerebral tissue 
      surrounding arteriovenous malformation.
PG  - 267-71
LID - 10.1111/j.1440-1789.2011.01249.x [doi]
AB  - We report an autopsy case of arteriovenous malformation (AVM) of the right 
      frontal lobe in a 50-year-old man, in whom post mortem examination revealed 
      massive tau deposition in the affected cerebral cortex. The patient was diagnosed 
      as having AVM at the age of 21 years, and died of unknown cause at the age of 50 
      years. Immunostaining with anti-phosphorylated tau antibody (AT8) revealed many 
      NFTs and neuropil threads, but not glial tau accumulation, in the right frontal 
      cortex surrounding the AVM. The NFTs and neuropil threads contained both 3-repeat 
      and 4-repeat tau. Ultrastructurally, the NFTs consisted of paired helical 
      filaments. In the other brain areas, a few NFTs were found in the parahippocampal 
      gyrus. There was no amyloid deposition in the brain. A variety of disease 
      conditions, including brain tumor, viral encephalitis, angioma and cervical 
      spondylotic myelopathy, have been reported to show Alzheimer-type NFTs. The 
      present findings indicate that abnormal tau deposition can occur in neurons, but 
      not in glial cells, of the affected cerebral cortex surrounding AVM.
