DP  - 2010 May
TI  - Intestinal transplantation in children: the first successful Italian series.
PG  - 1251-2
LID - 10.1016/j.transproceed.2010.03.046 [doi]
AB  - The preliminary experience of the first Italian program of pediatric intestinal 
      transplantation is presented herein. A multidisciplinary group with broad 
      experience in pediatric solid organ transplantation started the program. Nine 
      children with complications of chronic intestinal failure were listed for 
      transplantation. One child died on the waiting list; one received an isolated 
      liver transplantation; three isolated intestinal; three multivisceral; and one, a 
      combined liver/intestine transplantation. There was no in-hospital mortality, and 
      all children were weaned from parenteral nutrition. The recipient of the 
      multivisceral graft died after 14 months for unknown causes. All other recipients 
      are alive after a median follow-up of 13 months. Patient and graft actuarial 
      survivals for recipients of intestinal grafts were 100% at 1 year and 75% at 2 
      years.
