DP  - 2009
TI  - Immunity to Campylobacter: its role in risk assessment and epidemiology.
PG  - 1-22
LID - 10.1080/10408410802636017 [doi]
AB  - Acquired immunity is an important factor in the epidemiology of 
      campylobacteriosis in the developing world, apparently limiting symptomatic 
      infection to children of less than two years. However, also in developed 
      countries the highest incidence is observed in children under five years and the 
      majority of Campylobacter infections are asymptomatic, which may be related to 
      the effects of immunity and/or the ingested doses. Not accounting for immunity in 
      epidemiological studies may lead to biased results due to the misclassification 
      of Campylobacter-exposed but apparently healthy persons as unexposed. In risk 
      assessment studies, health risks may be overestimated when immunity is neglected.
