TI  - Sarcoidosis as risk factor for cryptococcal meningitis in an apparently 
      immunocompetent patient.
PG  - 33-5
LID - 10.1007/s10072-008-0856-y [doi]
AB  - Cryptoccus neoformans meningitis (CNM) is an opportunistic infection that 
      typically occurs in immunosuppressed patients. Subjects affected by sarcoidosis, 
      a systemic granulomatous disease of unknown cause, are predisposed to CNM because 
      of the impairment of cell-mediated immunity and because of the chronic 
      corticosteroid therapy they frequently receive. Here we report the case of a 
      38-year-old man who developed CNM as the first clinical manifestation of 
      sarcoidosis. The patient developed CNM even though he was apparently 
      immunocompetent and was not on therapy with either corticosteroid or cytotoxic 
      drugs.
