TI  - International classification of diseases codes showed modest sensitivity for 
      detecting community-acquired pneumonia.
PG  - 834-8
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To estimate the sensitivity of International Classification of 
      Diseases (ICD-9-CM) coding for detecting hospitalized community-acquired 
      pneumonia and to assess possible determinants for misclassification. STUDY DESIGN 
      AND SETTING: Based on microbiological analysis data, 293 patients with a 
      principal diagnosis of community-acquired pneumonia at seven hospitals in the 
      Netherlands were assigned to three categories (pneumococcal pneumonia, pneumonia 
      with other organism, or pneumonia with no organism specified). For these 
      patients, the assigned principal and secondary ICD-9-CM codes in the hospital 
      discharge record were retrieved and the corresponding sensitivity was calculated. 
      Furthermore, pneumonia-related patient characteristics were compared between 
      correctly and incorrectly coded subjects. RESULTS: The overall sensitivity was 
      72.4% for the principal code and 79.5% for combined principal and secondary 
      codes. For pneumococcal pneumonia (ICD-9-CM code 481) and pneumonia with 
      specified organism (ICD-9-CM code 482-483), the sensitivities were 35% and 18.3%, 
      respectively. Patient characteristics were not significantly different between 
      correctly and incorrectly coded subjects except for duration of hospital stay, 
      which correlated negatively with coding sensitivity (P=0.01). CONCLUSION: 
      ICD-9-CM codes showed modest sensitivity for detecting community-acquired 
      pneumonia in hospital administrative databases, leaving at least one quarter of 
      pneumonia cases undetected. Sensitivity decreased with longer duration of 
      hospital stay.
