VI  - 50
IP  - 2
DP  - 2005 Mar
TI  - Unexplained sudden death and the likelihood of drug abuse.
PG  - 419-22
AB  - The common history of drug abuse in adults with an undetermined cause of death 
      has led us to hypothesize that chronic drug abuse increases the risk of sudden 
      death. To begin evaluating this hypothesis, we conducted a retrospective 
      case-control study of 61 decedents whose cause of death remained undetermined 
      following autopsy matched one to one to a control group of pedestrians or 
      passengers killed in motor vehicle collisions. In 21 pairs, the case subject had 
      evidence of drug abuse but the control did not, and in 5 cases the reverse was 
      true. Analysis showed that individuals with an undetermined cause of death are 
      4.2 times more likely to have evidence of drug abuse than are victims of a motor 
      vehicle collision.
