TI  - Estimation of the misclassification rate of self-reported visual disability.
PG  - 367-71
AB  - PURPOSE: To estimate the misclassification rate of self-reported visual 
      disabilities in a hospital-based population with known visual impairment. 
      METHODS: Subjects (N=570) were recruited among patients aged 50 years and more 
      and classified to three categories of visual impairment level. The questionnaire 
      was administered to consenting patients through a telephone interview. Data 
      collected from questionnaires and medical records were compared regarding 
      severity of visual impairment. Sensitivity and specificity were determined for 
      each question. Predictive ability and misclassification rates were computed for 
      various prevalences. RESULTS: Questions related to near and far distance visual 
      acuity with glasses have both a good sensitivity (82.6% and 81.8%) and a good 
      specificity (85.6% and 88.9%) for the presence of severe visual impairment. 
      CONCLUSION: The findings allow the determination of the misclassification rate 
      and predictive ability. This could be useful to estimate the prevalence of visual 
      impairment from health surveys.
