DP  - 2002
TI  - Mild cognitive impairment: a clinically useful but currently ill-defined concept?
PG  - 405-10
AB  - The term mild cognitive impairment (MCI) has gained wide currency among 
      clinicians, and particularly dementia researchers, to denote patients with memory 
      deficits who do not yet fulfil the criteria for dementia, but are at high risk of 
      conversion. MCI is therefore regarded as the prodromic or pre-dementia stage of 
      Alzheimer's disease. The accurate categorization of these subjects has 
      far-reaching implications, both for research in this field and for those 
      individuals who fall within this diagnostic group. Despite a wealth of studies 
      examining the neuropsychological, neuroimaging and biological profiles of this 
      population, the characterization of MCI remains controversial. This brief 
      overview discusses a number of the issues related to this topic and questions the 
      currently accepted criteria.
