IS  - 0962-2802 (Print)
IS  - 0962-2802 (Linking)
VI  - 10
IP  - 5
DP  - 2001 Oct
TI  - Non-parametric comparison of relative versus cause-specific survival in 
      Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results (SEER) programme breast cancer 
      patients.
PG  - 339-52
AB  - Cancer-related mortality can be measured by two dissimilar methods: 
      cause-specific survival (based on mortality attributed to a specific cause), and 
      relative survival (based on mortality relative to a matched cohort). We used both 
      methods to determine actuarial survival in a population of 119,502 breast cancer 
      patients from the Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results (SEER) programme 
      data set, with 20 years of follow-up. The population was divided into four strata 
      by patient age and tumour stage. In all strata, there was only minimal deviation 
      between the two survival methods. Of particular interest was the cause-specific 
      treatment of patients recorded as dead of unknown cause, i.e. those deaths that 
      could not be attributed with certainty to either 'breast cancer' or to 'other 
      causes'. Findings suggest that the most reliable results may be obtained by 
      apportioning these deaths between 'dead of cause' and 'withdrawn at the time of 
      death'. This apportionment is based on the relative number of deaths attributed 
      to 'breast cancer' versus 'other causes'.
