TI  - Gastrointestinal carcinoid tumors: long-term prognosis for surgically treated 
      patients.
PG  - 1431-6
AB  - To evaluate long-term survival of patients with gastrointestinal carcinoid tumors 
      and to assess factors that may influence prognosis, 154 patients (49% females, 
      51% males), median age 62 years (range 12-84 years) treated at our institution 
      during 1972-1982 have been followed long term. Tumor location included the 
      foregut (7%), midgut (62%), and hindgut (30%). Ninety-five percent of the 
      patients underwent surgical or endoscopic excision of the primary tumor, with 
      overall operative mortality and postoperative morbidity rates of 2. 6% and 11%, 
      respectively. At follow-up, 60 patients (39%) were alive (median follow-up 18 
      years; range 1-26 years). The main causes of death included carcinoid tumor 
      burden (32%), unrelated causes (45%), other malignancy (19%), and unknown causes 
      (4%). Observed overall 5- and 10-year survivals were 69% and 53%, respectively. 
      Survival was not related to gender or symptoms at presentation. However, age, 
      embryologic origin, tumor size, depth of invasion, nodal status, and stage of 
      disease proved to be of statistical significance (log-rank). In a multivariate 
      Cox' model, only older age (> 62 years) [P = 0. 001, odds ratio (OR) = 3.4) and 
      embryologic origin (midgut versus foregut) (P = 0.045, OR = 0.45) provided 
      independent prognostic power when death from any cause was taken as the 
      end-point. This study confirms that patient's age and the site of the primary 
      tumor have prognostic significance. Carcinoid tumors are neuroendocrine tumors 
      with a relatively good prognosis, and long-term survival is possible despite 
      advanced stages of disease.
