DP  - 2000 Sep
TI  - Long-term morbidity and mortality after spinal cord injury: 50 years of 
      follow-up.
PG  - 563-6
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To determine the long-term mortality rate and the types of morbidity 
      among all people with spinal cord injuries (SCI) that occurred during the 1948 
      Israel War of Independence. METHOD: Chart review and telephone interviews for 
      collecting demographic data, injury characteristics, marital status, physical 
      activities, employment, morbidity and mortality. RESULTS: Twenty individuals with 
      SCI (19 males, one female). There was no regular follow-up during the first 20 
      years post injury. The most frequent morbidities were genito-urinary, 
      cardiovascular and decubiti. Ten (50%) had died during this overall follow-up 
      interval. The average age at death was 60 years. The cause of death was 
      cardiovascular in six, neoplastic disease in two, pneumonia in one, and one died 
      from an unknown cause. CONCLUSIONS: The data analysis showed that those who died 
      participated less in physical activity and fewer were employed as compared to the 
      survivors.
