TI  - [Malignant nodular hidradenoma--sweat gland tumor].
PG  - 683-6, 755
AB  - Malignant hidradenoma is a very rare tumor that originates from sweat glands. We 
      present a 61-year-old man with an ulcerated tumor in his right flank, 4 cm in 
      diameter, that was excised with a wide free margin. Histopathologic study showed 
      an ill-defined, epithelial neoformation, formed by lobules of clear polygonal 
      cells in the deep dermis and subcutaneous tissue, diagnosed as malignant nodular 
      hidradenoma. 1.5 years after excision there was enlargement of the right axillary 
      and inguinal lymph nodes, which showed metastatic, adnexal neoplastic cells. 
      Axillary resection and superficial dissection of the right inguinal nodes were 
      performed. After 3 months the tumor had spread to other lymph nodes and acute 
      obstructive renal failure required insertion of a pig-tail catheter into the 
      right ureter. Radiotherapy was followed by chemotherapy, but he died from 
      end-stage metastatic disease in multi-organ failure.
