TI  - Anaplastic osteosarcoma with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm and minimal osteoid 
      production.
PG  - 553-7
AB  - A case of osteosarcoma with unusual microscopic features, occurring in the right 
      proximal tibial metaphysis of a 12-year-old boy is reported. Radiographically, 
      the tumor was ill-defined and purely osteolytic. On gross examination, the tumor 
      was soft, fragile, spongy and red to brown in color. Microscopically, the tumor 
      consisted of pleomorphic cells possessing abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, 
      including cells larger than 100 microm in diameter. The cells were arranged in a 
      sheet, with few extracellular collagen fibers. Multiple sectioning of the 
      specimens revealed a small amount of osteoid production. Immunohistochemical 
      study revealed a positive reaction for vimentin and osteocalcin. Electron 
      microscopic study suggested the fibroblastic or osteoblastic origin of the cells.
