IP  - 5
DP  - 2000 May
TI  - Dynamic cardiomyoplasty: long-term clinical results in patients with dilated 
      cardiomyopathy.
PG  - 1445-7
AB  - BACKGROUND: Dynamic cardiomyoplasty has been considered to be an effective method 
      of surgical treatment of patients with end-stage heart failure, and is an 
      alternative to heart transplantation. METHODS: We critically evaluated the 
      long-term course of 52 patients with dilated cardiomyopathy who underwent dynamic 
      cardiomyoplasty and were followed-up for up to 110 months. RESULTS: Dilated 
      cardiomyopathy was due to undetermined cause in 42 patients (80.8%), Chagas 
      disease in 8 (15.4%), viral infection in 1 (1.9%), and peripartum cardiomyopathy 
      in 1 (1.9%). In the nonchagasic group the survival rates were 79.5% +/- 6.1%, 
      67.8% +/- 7.1%, 53.7% +/- 8.3%, 49.9% +/- 8.3%, 14.9% +/- 12.2%, and 14.9% +/- 
      12.2%, respectively, at 12, 24, 48, 60, 80 and 110 months of follow-up. In the 
      chagasic patients the survival rates were 37.5% +/- 17.1%, 12.5% +/- 11.7%, 12.5% 
      +/- 11.7% and 0%, respectively, at 12, 24, 48, and 60 months of follow-up, making 
      chagasic cardiomyopathy a possible contraindication for dynamic cardiomyoplasty. 
      CONCLUSIONS: There was no correlation between the clinical improvement and 
      hemodynamic data. Ventricular fibrillation was a frequent cause of immediate and 
      late death, suggesting the need for prophylactic use of antiarrhythmic drugs or 
      implantable cardioverter/ defibrillators.
