DP  - 2000 Jan
TI  - Occupational and residential magnetic field exposure and breast cancer in 
      females.
PG  - 24-9
AB  - The purpose of this study was to evaluate the effect of occupational magnetic 
      field exposure on breast cancer in females and to combine residential and 
      occupational magnetic field exposure to reduce misclassification. The study was 
      conducted as a case-control study within a population living within 300 meters of 
      transmission lines in Sweden. We identified cases of breast cancer in females 
      from the national cancer registry, and we selected one matched control per case 
      at random. Residential exposure was estimated through calculations of the 
      magnetic fields generated by power lines. We obtained information about 
      occupation from censuses, and the occupations were linked to a job-exposure 
      matrix that was based on magnetic field measurements. For occupational exposure 
      to magnetic fields over 0.25 microT closest in time before diagnosis, the 
      estimated relative risk was 1.0 [96% confidence interval (CI) = 0.6-1.7]. Women 
      below age 50 years at diagnosis had a relative risk of 1.5 (95% CI = 0.6-3.5). 
      For women below 50 years of age who had estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer, 
      there was a relative risk of 3.2 (95% CI = 0.5-18.9). The results for residential 
      and occupational exposures combined showed similar results.
