IS  - 0213-9111 (Linking)
VI  - 13
IP  - 5
DP  - 1999 Sep-Oct
TI  - [Competing death risks].
PG  - 399-406
AB  - The death of an individual is not a repetitive event, so if a cause of death 
      overtakes another cause in producing death, mortality rates from the overtaken 
      cause decrease. This phenomenon is known as competing risks of death and it must 
      be taken into account in any cause-specific mortality analysis. In this work the 
      competing risks concept is formalized and some historical data are described. The 
      main parametric tools to analyze competing risks are displayed, with a special 
      look at the Gompertz and Weibull functions. Regarding non-parametric models, the 
      Chiang method is shown and its applicability on both dependent and independent 
      causes of death is discussed. Finally, other tools specially useful in clinical 
      epidemiology are enumerated, including Cox regression, Kaplan-Meier and log-rank 
      methods, as well as the interactions between competing risks and 
      misclassification and selection biases.
