TI  - Nationwide maternal mortality in Surinam.
PG  - 55-9
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To assess the magnitude, causes and associated factors of maternal 
      mortality in Surinam. METHODS: Nationwide confidential enquiry. Maternal deaths 
      were identified using various methods and sources for the period 1991-1993. All 
      cases were examined by a maternal mortality expert committee for substandard care 
      analysis. RESULTS: The national maternal mortality ratio was 226 per 100,000 live 
      births, which is six times higher than the official maternal mortality ratio of 
      38 for the preceding three year period. The main causes of death were haemorrhage 
      (30%) and pre-eclampsia (20%). In 95% of analysed cases, substandard care factors 
      which had contributed to the deaths were present at one or more levels of 
      maternity care. CONCLUSIONS: Maternal mortality was found to be several times 
      higher than had been officially reported for Surinam during the previous three 
      decades. Improvement of maternity care services in Surinam is needed, and has to 
      be addressed at all levels, from the community, health centre and hospital to the 
      highest level of organisation.
