VI  - 87
IP  - 3
DP  - 1997 Mar
TI  - Violence and injury mortality in the Cape Town metropole.
PG  - 298-301
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To describe accurately the violence and injury mortality in a South 
      African city and demonstrate the utility of secondary data sources to identify 
      injury control priorities. DESIGN: Cross-sectional analysis of medicolegal 
      laboratory (state mortuary), forensic and police data. SETTING: Metropolitan Cape 
      Town, 1994. RESULTS: Non-natural causes (deaths due to homicide, suicide, 
      accidents and undetermined causes) accounted for almost 4000 deaths, which 
      comprised approximately 30% of all-cause mortality during 1994. The five main 
      violence and injury mortality categories were: homicide (1789 cases; 46% of all 
      non-natural mortality), transport accidents (1130 cases; 29% of all non-natural 
      mortality), fire (295 deaths; 8% of all non-natural mortality), suicide (291 
      deaths; 7% of all non-natural mortality) and drowning (96 cases; 2% of all 
      non-natural mortality). CONCLUSIONS: Priority issues in injury control include 
      the increasing homicidal and suicidal use of firearms, road and rail commuter 
      injury and the spatial distribution of injury. Surveillance, based on non-natural 
      mortality, should be included in local, regional and national health information 
      systems.
