DP  - 1995 Jul
TI  - Life expectancy in four U.S. racial/ethnic populations: 1990.
PG  - 350-5
AB  - Previous estimates of life expectancy in the United States have not corrected for 
      biases in population and mortality data, and no study has examined life 
      expectancy in U.S. Asian/Pacific Islander and American Indian populations. We 
      used information on population undercounts by race/ethnicity in the census and on 
      misclassification of race/ethnicity on death certificates to calculate life 
      expectancy for black, white, American Indian, and Asian men and women in the 
      United States in 1990. Correction for undercount and misclassification had little 
      effect on life expectancy estimates for whites, but it substantially decreased 
      estimates for American Indians and Asians. Asian men had life expectancies of 
      82.0 years and Asian women 85.8 years--the highest life expectancies reported for 
      any population in the world and beyond the limit predicted by some current 
      theories.
