VI  - 43
IP  - 10
DP  - 1993 Oct
TI  - Is the incidence of dementing illness changing? A 25-year time trend study in 
      Rochester, Minnesota (1960-1984).
PG  - 1887-92
AB  - We performed a time trend study of incidence of dementing illness in Rochester, 
      Minnesota. We ascertained age- and sex-specific incidence rates for the five 
      quinquennia, 1960 through 1984. The incidence rates sharply increased with 
      advancing age, reaching a high of 2,922/100,000 person years in the group 85 
      years and older. For dementia caused either solely or predominantly by 
      Alzheimer's disease, this figure was 2,600/100,000 person years for the oldest 
      age group. There were no significant differences in the incidence of dementing 
      illness between men and women. In the oldest age groups in the last two 
      quinquennia of study, there appears to be a trend toward increasing incidence 
      rates. Over the years, the proportion of cases attributed to dementia due to 
      unknown causes has decreased while the proportion of cases attributed to 
      Alzheimer's disease has increased.
