TI  - Mental health status among Puerto Ricans, Mexican Americans, and non-Hispanic 
      whites: the case of the misbegotten hypothesis.
PG  - 383-8; discussion 389-95
AB  - Challenged Shrout et al.'s (1992) comparisons of mental health characteristics of 
      island Puerto Ricans to Mexican Americans and non-Hispanic whites from the Los 
      Angeles Epidemiological Catchment Area Study. The hypothesis tested by Shrout et 
      al.--higher symptom counts but lower DSM-III prevalence rates among Puerto 
      Ricans--was misattributed to Rogler (1989). We also question the validity of 
      assessing lifetime prevalence and reaffirm the need for psychiatric 
      epidemiological research to consider the role of culture in diagnostic criteria.
