VI  - 114
IP  - 4
DP  - 1993 Apr
TI  - [Mortality from violent causes in the Americas].
PG  - 302-16
AB  - With a view to assembling information to aid in decision-making with regard to 
      prevention policies, a study of mortality from violent causes and its trends in 
      the countries of the Americas was carried out. The study focused on persons under 
      24 years of age and utilized information from 1980 and 1986 taken from the data 
      base of the Pan American Health Organization. The causes of death were grouped in 
      accordance with the International Classification of Diseases, Ninth Revision. 
      Accidental deaths were separated from intentional deaths by means of the 
      following classification: traffic accidents, other accidents, homicides, 
      suicides, and "unknown causes." The information on violent deaths was compared 
      with information on deaths from infectious diseases during the same period. The 
      results indicate that in 1986, 517,465 deaths from violent causes were recorded 
      in the 28 countries of the Region. Violent deaths as a proportion of total deaths 
      ranged from 3.7% in Jamaica to 26.8% in El Salvador. It was observed that between 
      1980 and 1986 traffic accidents and "other accidents" tended to diminish but 
      there was a moderate increase in suicides. Homicide rates varied markedly between 
      the countries. In the under-1 and 1-4 year age groups, the highest rates 
      corresponded to "other accidents," except in Chile, where among children aged 1-4 
      the largest proportion of deaths were attributed to "unknown causes." Comparative 
      analysis of deaths from violent causes and from infectious diseases in the 
      population aged 0-24 years showed that the former increase whereas the latter 
      decrease as age increases. The study pointed up the need to promote further 
      research on these phenomena in order to develop appropriate prevention 
      strategies.
