IS  - 1044-3983 (Linking)
VI  - 3
IP  - 5
DP  - 1992 Sep
TI  - The effects of nondifferential confounder misclassification in ecologic studies.
PG  - 456-9
AB  - In ecologic studies, covariate levels of groups are often quantified as the 
      prevalence of a dichotomous covariate. We show that, under certain conditions, 
      nondifferential misclassification of such a binary covariate does not reduce the 
      ability to control confounding by the covariate in ecologic studies. Thus, any 
      remaining exposure-disease association in an adjusted ecologic analysis cannot be 
      ascribed to incomplete control for confounding due to nondifferential 
      misclassification of the dichotomy under those conditions, although residual 
      confounding by the underlying covariate may still be present. This point is 
      illustrated by ecologic analyses of the association between population density 
      and mortality from lung cancer in women in 30 administrative districts of the 
      Federal Republic of Germany, in which control for cigarette smoking is required.
