VI  - 54
IP  - 8
DP  - 1991 Aug
TI  - Multiple sclerosis, tropical spastic paraparesis and HTLV-1 infection in 
      Afro-Caribbean patients in the United Kingdom.
PG  - 689-94
AB  - Forty four consecutive patients of Afro-Caribbean origin resident in the United 
      Kingdom (UK) were studied, based on a provisional diagnosis of myelopathy of 
      unknown cause, tropical spastic paraparesis (TSP) or multiple sclerosis (MS). Of 
      30 patients with progressive paraparesis 27 had serum antibodies to HTLV-1 and 
      were classified as having TSP. Fourteen patients fulfilled the criteria for MS 
      and none of 12 tested had HTLV-1 antibodies. All the TSP patients and nine of 
      those with MS were born in the West Indies. Five of the West Indianborn MS 
      patients had migrated to the UK after adolescence but the duration of residence 
      in the UK before symptoms of MS developed was similar to those born in the UK 
      (average 18 years). The features that differentiated MS from TSP patients, apart 
      from HTLV-1 status, included clinical evidence of cranial nerve involvement, more 
      extensive abnormalities on the brain and cervical cord MRI and asymmetry of the 
      VEP latency increase, all of which were more frequent in the MS group. Of the 
      three patients without a diagnosis one, born in the UK, had marked abnormalities 
      on MRI of the brain indistinguishable from those seen in MS.
