OID - PIP: 066758
OID - POP: 00223338
OAB - The conclusions of the detailed reporting of the journal O Dia on deaths of 
      children in 6 regional municipalities of Brazil are presented. The findings on 
      children up to age 2 are more complete than official statistics. For instance, 
      the most recent data from the State Secretary of Health referred to the first 4 
      months of 1989 based on reported cases. In contrast, the newspapers researched 
      the obituary records of 17 record-offices of Baixada Fluminense, verified the 
      registries of the major children's hospital of the region, collected data from 
      the Sao Salvador funerary home, checked the record books of the main cemeteries 
      where many children who were victims of starvation were buried. The results 
      showed that every 10 hours a child dies of hunger in Baixada. In the 17 
      record-offices and in the obituary records themselves hunger (diagnosed by 
      doctors as malnutrition) did not appear in explicit form; in the month of May, 
      1991, the books registered only 12 cases of child deaths caused by malnutrition. 
      In the majority of cases, they were entered with the notation: cause 
      undetermined. In May 1968, death certificates of children were issued with 
      undetermined cause of death. In almost 100% of these cases, the children died of 
      malnutrition at home, and even calling a doctor would not have provided a chance 
      of survival. The doctors register the cause as undetermined, as they are unable 
      to diagnose the cause. This artifice has been official since a resolution of the 
      State Secretary of Health was adopted in February, 1990. Fewer than 90% of 
      undetermined causes of death are attributable to malnutrition, according to the 
      director of the children's hospital of Nova Iguacu. The precise registration of 
      the death of children is not much in currency among poor families in Baixada, 
      although the parents often carry out the registration accurately. On the other 
      hand, the director of the community council of Nova Iguacu stated that although 
      the assessment by 0 Dia was more complete and up-to-date than official data, it 
      still did not completely reflect the real situation. There were children who did 
      not get into any kind of statistics and do not form part of any registry; those 
      dying at home who get buried at the farm. These are invisible deaths, as these 
      children are rarely registered when they are born.
