TI  - Certification of death.
PG  - 162-5
AB  - In Singapore, every deceased person must have a death certificate stating the 
      cause of death. The death certificates are prepared from certificates of cause of 
      death signed by medical practitioners, pathologists, coroners or inspectors of 
      death. The death certificate is a legal document and the person who certifies the 
      cause of death is responsible for the accuracy of the facts. Singapore has a 
      Coroner's system and all deaths due to unnatural, violent, accidental, sudden and 
      unknown causes must be reported to the coroner. These cases are usually subjected 
      to post-mortem examination after which pathologist will sign the certificate of 
      cause of death. Thus only pathologists are allowed to certify unnatural causes of 
      death. All other categories of personnel are allowed to certify natural causes 
      only. Any deviation will result in the certificate be rejected by the Registrar 
      of Death and the matter referred to the Coroner. The offending doctor would be 
      reprimanded by the court. Examples of wrong certification will be given and it is 
      hoped that medical practitioners will not make similar mistakes.
