VI  - 35
IP  - 2
DP  - 1989 Nov
TI  - Incidence of malignant fallopian tube tumors.
PG  - 236-9
AB  - Incidence rates were examined for epithelial malignant fallopian tube neoplasms 
      diagnosed between 1973 and 1984 and reported to nine population-based cancer 
      registries in the United States. The average annual incidence was 3.6 per million 
      women per year and there was no evidence of a change in the rate during the study 
      period. Age-specific incidence followed a pattern similar to that observed for 
      ovarian and endometrial neoplasms, rising rapidly during the reproductive years 
      and flattening out thereafter. The incidence rate varied only slightly by race, 
      with whites having a higher rate than blacks. Considerable variation in incidence 
      was observed among the registries. While this could have been due to true 
      regional differences in incidence, we cannot rule out the possibility that 
      misclassification of fallopian tube tumors as ovarian tumors was responsible. 
      Further investigations into the etiology of fallopian tube neoplasms should focus 
      on the role of reproductive factors that have previously been reported as risk 
      factors for ovarian and endometrial neoplasms.
