DP  - 1989 Nov
TI  - Eye injuries: a prospective survey of 5671 cases.
PG  - 888-94
AB  - 5671 patients with injuries presenting to a busy eye casualty department were 
      examined prospectively to determine the incidence, aetiology, and severity of the 
      injury. Of these cases 69.9% occurred at work, 18.3% during leisure and domestic 
      activities (excluding recognised sport), 2.3% during sport, and 1.9% were due to 
      assaults; contact lens injury occurred in a further 2.3%, and the cause was 
      unknown in 5.3%. One hundred and two (1.8%) patients required admission to 
      hospital, and of their injuries 13.7% occurred at work, 22.6% during leisure and 
      domestic activities, 42.2% at sport, 18.6% during assault, and 2.9% from an 
      unknown cause. Children under 10 years of age accounted for 4% of the total 
      patients presenting, but for more than 18% of those admitted and 26% of ocular 
      penetrations. 98.3% of all injuries involved periorbital or superficial ocular 
      structures only. The remainder involved intraocular structures. The majority of 
      serious injuries were contusional. There was only one case of intraocular foreign 
      body. This indicates that, while most injuries still occur in the workplace, 
      sport and leisure activities account for the majority of serious injuries. While 
      most injuries do not threaten sight, they do so disproportionately in children.
