LR  - 20161017
IS  - 0098-7484 (Print)
IS  - 0098-7484 (Linking)
VI  - 261
IP  - 12
DP  - 1989 Mar 24-31
TI  - Classification and analysis of fetal deaths in Massachusetts.
PG  - 1757-62
AB  - Fetal deaths, in contrast to infant deaths, have been subject to epidemiologic 
      analysis infrequently. We characterized 574 Massachusetts resident fetal deaths 
      from 1982 and assessed the accuracy of cause-of-death information available from 
      vital records compared with that from corresponding fetal autopsies. The fetal 
      death rate exceeded the neonatal mortality rate. Fetal mortality was higher among 
      black, unmarried, and older mothers. Fetuses of multiple-gestation pregnancies 
      had an unusually high risk of fetal death. Autopsy reports were obtained for 61% 
      of fetal deaths. The underlying cause of death from the fetal death record 
      differed from that on the autopsy report in 55% of cases. Systematic collection 
      of population-based autopsy data is a useful approach for improving the quality 
      and accuracy of mortality statistics on fetal deaths. Many stillbirths remain 
      unexplained, however, and research is needed to identify pathological markers 
      that might reduce the heterogeneity within the fetal deaths currently ascribed to 
      unknown causes.
