OID - PIP: 054528
OID - POP: 00181765
OAB - Reliable, accurate information is a prerequisite to achieving further reductions 
      in maternal mortality in the US and determining the risk factors for maternal 
      death. In 1988, the Centers for Disease Control (CDC) began a national pregnancy 
      mortality surveillance to detect all pregnancy-related deaths. The CDC will 
      conduct case investigations, compile data from both national and state vital 
      statistic reporting systems, and utilize investigative approaches that have been 
      successful in identifying misclassified maternal deaths. It is expected that the 
      number of deaths identified through the CDC system will be greater than that 
      reported by the vital statistics systems alone. Moreover, vital statistics 
      systems do not provide data sufficiently detailed (beyond only race and cause of 
      death) for epidemiological analysis. The success of CDC's abortion surveillance 
      system has been largely due to its use of multiple sources of reporting and 
      information. For example, of 538 possible abortion-related deaths identified by 
      CDC in 1972-82, only 69% were obtained from national statistics and state health 
      department reports; the remainder came from individuals and other sources such as 
      published case histories and medical and hospital committees. State vital 
      statistics are believed to understate maternal deaths by 17-73%.
