OID - PIP: 013832
OID - POP: 00118185
OAB - A study was undertaken of all perinatal deaths occurring in the Lagos University 
      Teaching Hospital from 1973-1977 to identify the major clinical causes of 
      perinatal mortality and to recommend measures to reduce or prevent such deaths. 
      Deaths were classified in 8 groups using a modified form of the Aberdeen 
      Classification for all stillbirths of 28 weeks gestation or more and all babies 
      born alive who died within 7 days. 636 perinatal deaths occurred out of 13,857 
      babies delivered, giving a perinatal mortality (PNM) of 45.9/1000 births. 72 
      perinatal deaths in 588 babies from multiple pregnancies were excluded from the 
      analysis, yielding a PNM of 42.5/1000 singleton births. The PNM/1000 singleton 
      births each year from 1973-77 was 34.6, 36.7, 45.5, 38.2, and 55.1. Of the 564 
      deaths to singleton babies from 1973-77, 172 (30.5%) were due to trauma, 131 
      (23.2%) to low birth weight, 77 (13.7%) to antepartum hemorrhage, 58 (10.3%) to 
      toxemia, 56 (9.9%) to unknown causes in mature babies, 24 (4.3%) to congenital 
      malformations, 23 (4.1%) to maternal disease, and 12 (2.1%) to other causes. In 
      11 cases (2%) there was insufficient data for determination of cause. 250 (39.3%) 
      of the recorded perinatal deaths were in unbooked patients. The stillbirth 
      rate/1000 singleton births from 1973-77 was 26.0, 26.8, 35.0, 30.0, and 42.6; the 
      average rate for the 5 years was 32.5/1000. 471 stillbirths occurred, including 
      431 to singletons. Although the data are from a hospital population, they are 
      believed to reflect the situations in Lagos and Nigeria.
